ation for writing this book well I  was it was interesting because one day  in our office I was I got a phone call  from Alex Haley who wrote roots and Alex  said to me you know I'd like to come  over and talk to you about representing  me and this was in about 1985 and and  we've been in business we're about four  years into our growth period and we were  doing well but we weren't doing great  but but our reputation was good because  we were taking care of those people that  had come with us and so I said he said  I'd like to come over to see you and I  said yeah when would you like to come  and he says if you look out your window  I'm in the telephone booth below so I  looked downstairs and there it was  waving at me so he came up and he used  to come to her office often and one of  the phrases he said was when roll person  dies  it's like a library burning and I didn't  quite get it at the time but as the time  went by I I understand what that 50  phrase meant that each life experiences  in each life are like pages in the book  so I started paying attention to the  people we represented not just  representing them but trying to get  interested in who they were  I had great relationships with these  people so it was easy for me to sit down  and just talk about family I could share  my experiences with my family and let  them share theirs so as the years passed  when I stopped working full-time I the  idea was in my head that I wanted to  capture some of the stories that I had  heard over the years so I asked a number  of people if I could interview them to  get the facts correct I taped the  conversation I spent about two and a  half years writing the book and the book  is about the stories the powerful  influences and defining moments in their  lives the turning points in their life  is it's not about you know unsuccessful  because I did this at the State  Department of the White House or I did  this on the playing field but what was  the personal thing in your l